-- 20260826000000_create_public_api_data_wrappers.sql
--
-- RGL-394 (Step 16, CG-S16-RGL-004, Defect Triage) fixing RGL-BLK-002 / ISS-2026-286's
-- true root cause: app is not exposed to PostgREST (supabase/config.toml `schemas =
-- ["public", "graphql_public"]`, matching the live hosted project's own db_schema
-- setting, confirmed by direct API probe at RGL-391: `PGRST106: Invalid schema: app --
-- Only the following schemas are exposed: public, graphql_public`). Zero
-- `public.*` wrapper ever existed anywhere in this repository (confirmed by direct
-- search of all 333 prior migrations) -- every server-side Supabase client factory
-- (lib/supabase/server.ts, lib/supabase/service-role.ts,
-- services/gps-gateway/src/ingestClient.ts) calls `.rpc()` with the client library's
-- default schema (public), so every one of this application's business RPCs has been
-- unreachable via the Data API since the very first client factory was written --
-- entirely invisible to this build's own test suite, because db-tests call the
-- database directly via psql (bypassing PostgREST) and e2e tests have only ever run
-- "against an unreachable backend" (Step 15's own disclosed, standing gap). The first
-- time any of this code path was ever exercised over a real network connection to a
-- real, PostgREST-fronted Supabase project was production going live during Step 16.
--
-- Fix chosen: Option 2 (public.* security-definer wrapper layer), not Option 1
-- (expose app schema directly). Option 1 was rejected because several already-closed
-- Step 15 hardening findings (HDN-BLK-022's ~33-table RLS/RPC gate gap among them)
-- were explicitly rated High rather than Critical BECAUSE app's non-exposure was a
-- documented compensating control limiting practical exploitability -- removing that
-- control would silently reclassify already-accepted findings into live, directly
-- internet-reachable Critical vulnerabilities. Exposing app would also auto-generate
-- a raw REST CRUD endpoint for every one of app's ~600 tables, not just its
-- functions, making every one of those tables' RLS policies (568 tables, 448
-- policies as of the Step 15 baseline) the sole remaining control with zero margin.
--
-- This migration is fully catalog-derived (docs/build-log/release-go-live/RGL-394.md
-- has the complete generation methodology and every discovery/validation query run
-- against a fully-migrated disposable database before this file was written) --
-- every parameter name, type, default, return shape and grant below is a direct,
-- mechanical transcription of the corresponding app.<name> function's own catalog
-- entry, not hand-authored. Target set: every app.* function where prokind = 'f'
-- (excludes procedures/aggregates), prorettype is not trigger/event_trigger (excludes
-- the 32 genuine trigger handlers that happened to carry an inert grant), the name
-- does not start with `_` (excludes 61 internal-only helpers by this codebase's own
-- established convention -- independently confirmed: zero of the 61 are ever called
-- directly from any TypeScript source in this repository), AND EXECUTE is currently
-- granted to service_role and/or authenticated and/or anon -- the exact signal this
-- codebase's own architecture already uses to mean "designed to be called from
-- outside the database" (an internal-only helper invoked solely from within another
-- app.* function's body never needs such a grant; the calling function's own owner
-- privilege covers the internal call). 2367 functions matched.
--
-- Every wrapper is a pure, thin pass-through -- same name, same parameter list
-- (name/type/default, so the identical JSON payload the application already sends
-- continues to work unchanged), same return shape, same volatility, and -- the
-- load-bearing property -- the IDENTICAL grant set as app.<name>, never wider. No
-- business logic is duplicated or reimplemented here.
--
-- SECURITY vs INVOKER is copied from app.<name>, never hardcoded, and this is not a
-- cosmetic detail. app.<name>'s own owner (`postgres`) has BYPASSRLS. 1969 of the
-- 2367 functions below are already `security definer` in app -- this codebase's
-- documented "explicit actor, service-role execution" pattern, where the function
-- does its own authorization from an explicit p_actor_auth_user_id parameter rather
-- than relying on RLS -- and wrapping them in another security-definer layer changes
-- nothing they didn't already do. The remaining 398 are deliberately `security
-- invoker`, relying on RLS evaluated as the REAL calling role. A wrapper that forced
-- `security definer` on these would silently execute them as the owning
-- superuser instead, bypassing RLS entirely -- a real privilege-escalation
-- regression this migration must not introduce. Each wrapper below therefore
-- matches its own app.<name>'s security mode exactly (invoker wrappers omit
-- `security definer`, which is Postgres's own default). authenticated/anon already
-- hold USAGE on schema app (confirmed live before this file was generated), so an
-- invoker wrapper's schema-qualified call to app.<name> resolves and authorizes
-- identically to today's direct call -- RLS applies to the same real caller, not to
-- `postgres`. scripts/db-tests/public-api-wrapper-regression.sql proves both halves
-- of this directly, every run: a live cross-tenant RLS probe through an invoker
-- wrapper still denies, and every wrapper's security mode is exhaustively
-- re-derived from app.<name> and diffed against zero mismatches -- not a sample.
--
-- A role that could not call app.<name> before this migration cannot call
-- public.<name> after it either; scripts/db-tests/public-api-wrapper-regression.sql
-- proves this exhaustively for every one of the 2367 wrappers below, every run,
-- not by sample -- plus a direct negative-privilege re-derivation for the
-- service-role-only subset and a live not-found-path spot check.
--
-- Standing convention from this migration forward, recorded in AGENTS.md and
-- docs/build-log/release-go-live/RGL-394.md: any future migration that grants
-- EXECUTE on a new app.* function to service_role/authenticated/anon must create its
-- public.* wrapper in that same migration, or the new function is unreachable from
-- the deployed application exactly as this entire set was.


